WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:17.462
BEN: Hello, Little Fox readers, and welcome to People in the News , a new biography show just

2
00:00:17.463 --> 00:00:23.954
for kids! Each week we'll introduce you to an interesting person who has accomplished amazing things in a

3
00:00:23.955 --> 00:00:29.029
variety of fields such as business, science, sports, entertainment, and politics. Today Olivia is going to tell you about

4
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:37.451
Jackie Chan—an actor from Hong Kong who's famous for performing death-defying stunts. Here's Olivia with her fascinating report

5
00:00:37.452 --> 00:00:42.345
on this very successful movie star. OLIVIA: Thanks, Ben. Well, it's pretty incredible to think that when Jackie

6
00:00:42.346 --> 00:00:55.937
Chan was born in Hong Kong on April 7, 1954, his parents were so poor, they had to

7
00:00:55.938 --> 00:01:02.334
borrow money to pay for his birth. Jackie's father believed that learning kung fu would help Jackie to

8
00:01:02.335 --> 00:01:07.570
be strong, patient, and brave. He woke Jackie up early every morning, and the two of them practiced together.

9
00:01:07.571 --> 00:01:17.739
When Jackie was seven, his parents enrolled him at the China Drama Academy to learn the art of

10
00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:27.655
Peking Opera, a form of traditional Chinese theater. Once really popular throughout much of China, it involves singing,

11
00:01:27.656 --> 00:01:39.978
acting, acrobatics, and martial arts—and a lot of difficult training. Students spent about 19 hours every day practicing

12
00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:48.964
physically demanding tasks like running while balancing cups of water and holding difficult martial arts positions for a

13
00:01:48.965 --> 00:01:53.795
long time. At the China Drama Academy students were given very little food, and if they made any mistakes,

14
00:01:53.796 --> 00:01:57.799
they were beaten severely. On top of all that, the students weren't even taught to read or write

15
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:06.957
at the school. Those were things Jackie had to teach himself when he got older. By the time

16
00:02:06.958 --> 00:02:11.772
Jackie left the China Drama Academy, it was almost impossible to find a job performing Peking Opera, which

17
00:02:11.773 --> 00:02:17.954
was no longer very popular. But his training at school had given him all the skills he needed

18
00:02:17.955 --> 00:02:24.421
to be a stuntman. Along with some of his former classmates, Jackie started working in Hong Kong's film

19
00:02:24.422 --> 00:02:30.761
industry. Before long he became known for his willingness to do just about any stunt you can imagine. Within

20
00:02:30.762 --> 00:02:35.417
a few years, Jackie wasn't just performing stunts for movies—he was acting in them too. But Jackie soon

21
00:02:35.418 --> 00:02:40.851
became frustrated with the way kung fu films were made. The films were similar to one another, and

22
00:02:40.852 --> 00:02:48.414
their tone was always very serious. You might be surprised to hear this about a guy who performs

23
00:02:48.415 --> 00:02:59.482
fight scenes, Ben, but Jackie actually hates violence. He does, however, love comedy, so he decided to try

24
00:02:59.483 --> 00:03:07.817
something new: put humor into his movies by using unusual props, such as ladders or chairs, as weapons. Jackie

25
00:03:07.818 --> 00:03:17.636
was also willing to look silly in his movies, rubbing his head after getting hit or looking too

26
00:03:17.637 --> 00:03:24.628
tired to keep fighting. The first movies to feature Jackie doing this kind of physical comedy were Snake

27
00:03:24.629 --> 00:03:30.134
in the Eagle's Shadow and Drunken Master . The fight scenes in these movies weren't just action-packed; they

28
00:03:30.135 --> 00:03:38.467
were really funny, and audiences loved them! With his unique blend of action and comedy, Jackie was soon

29
00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:47.445
a huge star in Asia. The success of movies like Rush Hour and Shanghai Noon turned Jackie into a

30
00:03:47.446 --> 00:03:54.763
star in Hollywood too. Another thing that makes Jackie's movies popular, and sets them apart from other movies,

31
00:03:54.764 --> 00:04:01.031
is the unbelievable stunt work that Jackie does himself. He has jumped onto a moving helicopter, slid down

32
00:04:01.032 --> 00:04:05.926
the side of a building, roller-skated over a moving car, even jumped out of an airplane without a

33
00:04:05.927 --> 00:04:14.179
parachute, landing on top of a hot-air balloon! BEN: Whoa! It sounds like Jackie Chan isn't afraid of

34
00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:28.075
anything! OLIVIA: Actually, Jackie has admitted that sometimes he's very scared before trying a stunt. And he has good

35
00:04:28.076 --> 00:04:33.495
reason to be afraid. Over the years he has dislocated lots of body parts—including his shoulder, pelvis, and

36
00:04:33.496 --> 00:04:40.503
sternum—and crushed his legs between two cars. One time he was even slashed by a sword! Another time

37
00:04:40.504 --> 00:04:47.160
he broke his foot and had to wear a cast. But guess what, Ben. Jackie had the cast

38
00:04:47.161 --> 00:04:52.308
covered and painted to look like his sneaker and continued filming that same day. His worst injury happened

39
00:04:52.309 --> 00:04:57.798
in 1986, while he was filming Armour of God in Yugoslavia. Jackie was jumping off a castle wall, down

40
00:04:57.799 --> 00:05:05.884
to a tree branch. He did the jump fine, but he decided to do it again, to try

41
00:05:05.885 --> 00:05:13.696
to make it look even better. The second time he did the jump, he missed the branch and

42
00:05:13.697 --> 00:05:17.113
fell to the ground, landing headfirst and fracturing his skull. That injury nearly killed Jackie, leaving him with

43
00:05:17.114 --> 00:05:24.874
a hole in his skull as well as a partial hearing loss in one ear. One good thing

44
00:05:24.875 --> 00:05:29.943
came from this terrible event though. While recovering, Jackie realized that, despite his fame, he hadn't done much

45
00:05:29.944 --> 00:05:35.251
to make the world a better place. Remembering his own poor childhood, he decided to start helping kids. Two

46
00:05:35.252 --> 00:05:41.098
years later the Jackie Chan Charitable Foundation was born. This foundation provides scholarships for young people interested in

47
00:05:41.099 --> 00:05:45.768
the performing or technical arts and helps people who are suffering from natural disasters and serious illnesses. In

48
00:05:45.769 --> 00:05:53.999
2004 Jackie started the Dragon's Heart Foundation, which raises money to build schools in remote areas of China.

49
00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.324
Jackie brings just as much energy and enthusiasm to these charities as he does to his movies. He

50
00:06:00.325 --> 00:06:15.581
is often very busy visiting children and attending fundraisers. BEN: No wonder Jackie Chan is a person in the

51
00:06:15.582 --> 00:06:23.587
news: he's a pretty cool guy! Thanks for telling us more about him, Olivia. Well, that's it for

52
00:06:23.588 --> 00:06:33.784
this week, Little Fox readers. We'll see you next week, when we meet another person in the news!

